INT. RUINED BREWERY CELLAR - NIGHT

Vaulted brick, half collapsed. Copper vats stand dented and silent. Dust sifts from the ceiling with every distant shell.

SERGEANT HOLLIS BRANNIGAN (30s, helmet strapped, face grey with plaster) crouches behind a barrel, rifle across his knee. Opposite him, PRIVATE TOMAS KEPLER (19, glasses cracked at one corner) huddles with a radio set strapped to his back.

BRANNIGAN
Kepler, get me headquarters.

KEPLER
Trying, Sarge.

He turns a dial. Static, then a thread of music from far away. He jerks back.

KEPLER (CONT'D)
That's a German station.

BRANNIGAN
Turn it off.

KEPLER
I can't, it won't--

A shell lands overhead. Dust pours. Brannigan drags Kepler under a vat. They crouch, shoulders pressed, breathing hard.

BRANNIGAN
Listen. When this stops, we move. You first.

KEPLER
Me?

BRANNIGAN
You've got the radio.

Kepler nods. Swallows. His hands shake. Brannigan notices, says nothing, lays his own hand over the boy's for a moment.

Footsteps on the stairs. Both men freeze. Rifles up.

A figure descends. Hands raised. Torn coat. CAPTAIN LUCIEN DUVAL (40s, French officer, bandage around his head) enters from the shadows, a lantern in one fist.

DUVAL
Do not shoot. I am on your side.

BRANNIGAN
Prove it.

DUVAL
(in English, slowly)
Vive la France.

KEPLER
That's not proof.

BRANNIGAN
^Show me your tags.

DUVAL
Show me yours, American.

Silence. Duval lowers the lantern. Dust floats between the three men. Slowly, Brannigan lowers the rifle.

BRANNIGAN
Sit down, Captain. You look half dead.

Duval sits against a vat. Draws a flask. Offers it to Kepler.

DUVAL
Beer. From this very cellar. Before the war.

Kepler stares. Takes it. Drinks. Coughs. A laugh bursts out of him, wet and astonished.

Overhead, the shelling resumes, closer. The three men look up as one.

BRANNIGAN
On three, we go.

The lantern flame gutters.
